<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1744 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Tu scendi dalle stelle</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A widely sung Christmas carol from Italy, written in 1744 by a Neapolitan priest. It is often played on the zampogna, a big Italian bagpipe.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Alphonsus Liguori</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Alphonsus Liguori</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Italian Catholics; traditionally the bagpipers (zampognari) of southern Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Deliceto, province of Foggia, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1744</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and melody by Alphonsus Liguori, written in 1744)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-scendi-dalle-stelle">Tu scendi dalle stelle</a>&quot; (&quot;You Come Down from the Stars&quot;) is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, written in 1744 by Alphonsus Liguori in the musical style of a pastorale. Liguori was a prominent Neapolitan priest and scholastic philosopher, later canonised, who founded the Redemptorist missionary order. He wrote both the melody and the original words. By one account he wrote the song while staying at the Convent of the Consolation, one of his order's houses in the small city of Deliceto, in the province of Foggia in south-eastern Italy, and called it &quot;Little song to Child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>&quot;.</p><p>The Italian song came after an earlier one that Liguori had written in Neapolitan, &quot;Quanno nascette Ninno&quot; (&quot;When the child was born&quot;), entitled &quot;For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>' birth&quot; and sometimes called the &quot;Carol of the Bagpipers&quot; (Canzone d'i zampognari). The Italian &quot;Little song&quot; became a widely popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It is found in many arrangements and is commonly sung, but it is traditionally associated with the zampogna, the large Italian bagpipe.</p><p>The earliest known sound recording was made by the Italian baritone Rodolfo Fornari, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> Opera Company, on 21 September 1915 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City for Columbia Records, under the title &quot;Novena Di Natale&quot;. A year later an unknown ensemble and singer recorded it for Columbia, though the record was not released until 1917, and on 24 September 1917 the Italian-American tenor Pasquale Feis recorded it for Victor as &quot;Pastorale Di Natale&quot;. In later decades recordings began to use the original title, crediting various composers and arrangers, among them Liguori and Tommaso Capocci, or calling the song traditional. An edition published in 1932 by A. Paolilli's Music Co. of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providence-rhode-island">Providence, Rhode Island</a>, credits the music to Capocci and the words to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> and does not mention the earlier work.</p><p>The tune has been used by other composers. Domenico Scarlatti used it in his Sonata in C major, Kk513, and Ottorino Respighi took the carol for &quot;Adoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>&quot;, the second movement of his Trittico botticelliano (1927). Luigi Tutela composed a &quot;Novena Di Natal&quot; that closely resembles Liguori's tune and words; the Reverend Francis Auriemma recorded it for Victor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City on 19 September 1916. Anthony Velona and Remo Capra set English words to the music as &quot;O Bambino&quot;, also known as &quot;One Cold and Blessed Winter&quot;, which several singers recorded in the late 1960s, before the history of the song was widely known. The Italian-American tenor Sergio Franchi recorded the traditional Italian version for Durium and again, in a slightly updated arrangement, on his RCA Victor album The Heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> (Cuor' Di Natale) of 1965. The Italian composer Piero Niro wrote Three Variations on &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-scendi-dalle-stelle">Tu scendi dalle stelle</a>&quot; for large orchestra (2000), and Ralph Woodward has arranged the carol for unaccompanied choir. There are several English translations in the style of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Bible</a>, and at least one in modern English.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song addressed to the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, who has come down from heaven to be born in the cold of a winter night. The singer wonders that the maker of the world should lie poor and without warmth, and sees in it how much the child's love has cost him. The mood is tender and full of pity.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Italian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Italian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Tu scendi dalle stelle, o Re del cielo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You come down from the stars, O King of heaven,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">e vieni in una grotta al freddo e al gelo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and come into a cave, in the cold and the frost,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">e vieni in una grotta al freddo e al gelo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and come into a cave, in the cold and the frost.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O Bambino mio divino,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O my divine Child,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">io ti vedo qui tremar;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I see you trembling here;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">o Dio beato!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O blessed God!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Ahi quanto ti costò l'avermi amato!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah, how much it cost you to have loved me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Ahi quanto ti costò l'avermi amato!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah, how much it cost you to have loved me!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">A te, che sei del mondo il Creatore,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To you, who are the Creator of the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">mancâro panni e foco, o mio Signore,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>clothes and fire were lacking, O my Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">mancâro panni e foco, o mio Signore.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>clothes and fire were lacking, O my Lord.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Caro eletto pargoletto,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Dear chosen little child,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">quanto questa povertà</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>how much this poverty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">più m'innamora,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>makes me love you the more,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">giacché ti fece amor povero ancora,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>since love made you poor as well,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">giacché ti fece amor povero ancora.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>since love made you poor as well.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 56 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Tu lasci il bel gioir del divin seno,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You leave the fair joy of the divine bosom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">per venir a penar su questo fieno,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to come and suffer on this hay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">per venir a penar su questo fieno.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to come and suffer on this hay.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Dolce amore del mio core,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sweet love of my heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">dove amor ti trasportò?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where has love carried you?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O Gesù mio,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O my Jesus,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">perché tanto patir? Per amor mio!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why suffer so much? For love of me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">perché tanto patir? Per amor mio!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why suffer so much? For love of me!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Ma se fu tuo voler il tuo patire,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But if your suffering was your own will,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">perché vuoi pianger poi, perché vagire?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why then do you wish to weep, why to wail?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">perché vuoi pianger poi, perché vagire?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why then do you wish to weep, why to wail?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Sposo mio, amato Dio,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My spouse, beloved God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">mio Gesù, t'intendo sì!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my Jesus, yes, I understand you!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Ah, mio Signore,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah, my Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">tu piangi non per duol, ma per amore,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you weep not for pain, but for love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">tu piangi non per duol, ma per amore.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you weep not for pain, but for love.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Tu piangi per vederti da me ingrato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You weep to see yourself by me, the ungrateful one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">dopo sì grande amor, sì poco amato,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>after so great a love, so little loved,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">dopo sì grande amor, sì poco amato!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>after so great a love, so little loved!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O diletto del mio petto,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O delight of my breast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">se già un tempo fu così,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if once it was so,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">or te sol bramo:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now I long for you alone:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">caro non pianger più, ch'io t'amo e t'amo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dear one, weep no more, for I love you and love you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">caro non pianger più, ch'io t'amo e t'amo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dear one, weep no more, for I love you and love you.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Tu dormi, Ninno mio, ma intanto il core</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You sleep, my little one, but meanwhile your heart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">non dorme, no, ma veglia a tutte l'ore,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>does not sleep, no, but keeps watch at every hour,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">non dorme, no, ma veglia a tutte l'ore.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>does not sleep, no, but keeps watch at every hour.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Deh, mio bello e puro Agnello,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah, my fair and pure Lamb,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">a che pensi? dimmi tu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what are you thinking of? Tell me.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O amore immenso,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O boundless love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">&quot;Un dì morir per te&quot; – rispondi – &quot;io penso&quot;,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Of dying for you one day,&quot; you answer, &quot;I am thinking&quot;,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">&quot;Un dì morir per te&quot; – rispondi – &quot;io penso&quot;.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Of dying for you one day,&quot; you answer, &quot;I am thinking&quot;.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Dunque a morire per me, tu pensi, o Dio</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So you think of dying for me, O God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ed altro, fuor di te, amar poss'io?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and can I love anything else but you?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ed altro, fuor di te, amar poss'io?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and can I love anything else but you?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O Maria, speranza mia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Mary, my hope,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">s'io poc'amo il tuo Gesù,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if I love your Jesus little,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">non ti sdegnare</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>do not be angry:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">amalo tu per me, s'io nol so amare!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>love him for me, if I do not know how to love him!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">amalo tu per me, s'io nol so amare!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>love him for me, if I do not know how to love him!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Alphonsus Liguori (1744); as quoted in Italian Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tu_scendi_dalle_stelle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>Line 1.2: grotta, a cave or grotto: in Italian tradition the stable of the Nativity is a cave.</li><li>Line 2.2: mancâro, an old form of mancarono, 'were lacking'.</li><li>Line 4.4: Sposo mio, 'my spouse' or 'my bridegroom': the soul speaks to Christ as its bridegroom, an old image of Christian devotion.</li><li>Lines 5.1-5.2: the Italian word order is free: 'you weep at seeing yourself so little loved by me, ungrateful, after so great a love'.</li><li>Line 6.1: Ninno, a Neapolitan word for a baby or little boy.</li><li>Line 6.1: il core, 'the heart', here the child's own.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
