<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sonata in C major, Kk513 (Domenico Scarlatti)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">uses the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Novena Di Natal (Luigi Tutela)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">recorded 1916</span><span class="sg-chain-h">song closely resembling Liguori's tune and words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Trittico botticelliano, second movement, &quot;Adoration of the Magi&quot; (Ottorino Respighi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1927</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the carol adopted in the movement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O Bambino, also known as One Cold and Blessed Winter (Anthony Velona and Remo Capra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English words set to the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Three Variations on &quot;Tu scendi dalle stelle&quot; (Piero Niro)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2000</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations for large orchestra</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement for unaccompanied choir (Ralph Woodward)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Quanno nascette Ninno (Alphonsus Liguori)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the earlier song in Neapolitan, after which the Italian one was written</span></li></ul></section>
